--- body ---
## Context

Found by the step-3 sweep after the ticket-0875 hunt merged (PR #1133,
2026-10-02). The blocker that review caught: the guard's suite-wide
`export BASH_ENV=` exemption was decided by `grep -E` re-parsing the
`\x01`-delimited stream the awk state machine had built — a cross-tool byte
contract that produced a BAD verdict on one CI runner allocation and EXEMPT
on another, on identical bytes and tool versions (GNU Awk 5.2.1, grep 3.11,
bash 5.2.21 printed by the diagnostic cycle). The fix moved the exemption
into the same read loop that scans the file.

Two members of the same class remain in tests/test_bash_tests_are_hermetic.sh:

1. **Verdict-deciding (this ticket's target):** the UNTERMINATED check at
   line 333 — `printf '%s\n' "$ll" | grep -qE -- '^-1'"$LL"` — still greps the
   awk-built stream. The same runner nondeterminism, if it strikes this
   pattern, makes the guard answer UNTERMINATED for a healthy file
   (fail-loud, but a confusing false verdict that would mask the real
   answer).
2. Diagnostic-only (record, not blocking): the `diag_tok` probe at line 595
   re-parses the stream the same way, but it only decorates the FAIL message.

## Actions

1. Move the UNTERMINATED detection into the same awk pass that builds the
   logical-lines stream (emit a sentinel token, e.g. a `-1`-prefixed record,
   consumed by the read loop) — one implementation, no second tool over the
   raw stream, mirroring the merged exemption fix.
2. Red control first: a fixture whose heredoc heuristic loses track must
   still answer UNTERMINATED after the change.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] No verdict-deciding path in the guard re-parses the awk-built stream
      with grep; the UNTERMINATED check consumes the same read loop
- [ ] A red control pins UNTERMINATED detection before the fix and passes
      after

## Plan annotation (raid)

Actions (all in tests/test_bash_tests_are_hermetic.sh, sole file):

1. Control 0q (red-able): fixture with `export BASH_ENV=` BEFORE an unterminated
   heredoc opening; expects UNTERMINATED. Fails as "expected 'UNTERMINATED',
   got 'EXEMPT'" iff the exemption break survives the fix.
2. Control 0r (green-stay-green): plain unterminated heredoc, no exemption;
   expects UNTERMINATED before and after — pins the sentinel round-trip
   (awk END emission, \001 field split, -1 recognition).
3. Control-range mentions updated (0c)-(0p) -> (0c)-(0r) in the two catalog
   spots plus the section intro's class list.
4. Remove the grep block (printf | grep -qE -- '^-1'"$LL" ...) at 333-336.
5. Sentinel branch right after the `[ -n "$lineno" ] || continue` guard:
   lineno="-1" -> printf UNTERMINATED, return 0. lineno is NR-based (>= 1),
   the sentinel splits as lineno="-1", s="", u="".
6. Remove the exemption `break` (keep exempt=1) so the loop reaches the
   sentinel; comment explains why the break must not come back.
7. Header claim rewritten, scoped to verdict-deciding paths; diag_tok (the
   independent cross-check by the distrusted tool) stays.
8. Mechanical post-check: `grep -n 'grep.*"$ll"'` on the guard must return
   nothing; scan-output byte-diff vs post-controls baseline allows only the
   two new PASS lines.

First test: 0q/0r run via the guard file itself (integration tier;
check-fast does not run it). Red sequence: controls green -> apply grep
removal + sentinel branch only -> 0q reds live (the hazard demonstrated) ->
apply break removal -> green.

Dependencies: none (no external consumer of _file_verdict/_logical_lines;
no other test expects UNTERMINATED; no .idh-checks.json so the PR gate is
the full make check).

Tautology scan: 0q discriminates exactly the break (code path walked);
0r catches branch-omitted and broken-condition but not placement; neither
detects fix-not-applied — that is the action-8 mechanical sweep's job.
